window.docContents[195].resolve({"/Lexer-and-grammar/Abstract-syntax-and-parser/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Abstract-syntax-and-parser":{"contents":"The abstract syntax, the tree form of the grammar of , has one inductive type per class of nonterminals. Ty has the basic types, class, pointer, vector and function types and the internal type of nullptr. Expr has literals, nullptr, variable, unary and binary operators, conditional, call, the two forms of new, field access, arrow, dereference, indexing, the lambda and the call of a function value. A Param may be by reference. Cmd has block, if, while, for, return, declaration with a type or with auto, assignment and expression statement. A Fun has a return type, a name, parameters and a body. A ClassDecl has a qualified name, an optional base, fields and methods with their visibility, at most one constructor and at most one destructor. Expr also has this, the method call and the new with arguments, and Cmd has delete. A Program is a list of declarations. The abstract grammar of the implemented subset follows, with one alternative per constructor of Syntax.lean and in the same order, the constructor named in the right column.Here  is an integer,  a boolean,  a variable identifier and  a function identifier. The abstract syntax has no parentheses and no precedence, because the tree fixes the structure.\n\nOne parser function per expression nonterminal, from Expr down to Primary. Each level of the grammar is one level of precedence, and the repetition ( op Operand )* builds a left associative tree. In the implemented subset PostfixExpr covers the function call, indexing, field access and arrow, UnaryExpr adds the dereference, and Primary covers literals, nullptr, variables, parenthesised expressions and the two forms of new. Lambdas are parsed as argument expressions, argExpr, in the three positions the grammar gives them. Method calls and this are not parsed yet.\n\nOne parser function per command nonterminal. The function statement chooses the production by the first token, and exprStatement reads an expression and then decides between assignment and expression statement by the presence of =. In the implemented subset Type is a basic type, a class name optionally followed by *, std::vector<Type> optionally followed by *, or std::function<Type(Type, …)>. A local declaration may be a reference, and its initialiser may be a lambda.\n\nA program is a sequence of declarations up to eof, each a class with public fields or a function. The function runParser runs the lexer and a parser on a string and requires the whole input to be consumed. Namespaces, templates, methods and constructors are not parsed yet.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Lexer and grammar","header":"1.3. Abstract syntax and parser","id":"/Lexer-and-grammar/Abstract-syntax-and-parser/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Abstract-syntax-and-parser"}});